      "widgets_values": [
        "# Pulse Slate 3 · cast and references\n\n**The feature the pack is named for.** Three images sit in the Asset Bin, each\nwith a **description** and a **retention** value, and every one is cited in the\nprose by `@Name` — never by an ordinal.\n\n## Why you never type `<Picture 2>`\n\nThe tag is assigned from *live bin order* at compile time, and MiniMax's\ntokenizer numbers by socket position: adding a reference or dragging one up the\nlist renumbers everything after it. A hand-typed ordinal goes stale the moment\nyou do that, silently. Write `@Mimi`; the compiler writes the number.\n\nA description does one more thing: it promotes the reference to a `<Subject N>`\ndefinition, which is the shape MiniMax's own reference format asks for. Clear the\ndescription and the same image is cited as a bare `<Picture i>` instead. Both are\nlegal; the first is what identity consistency is built on.\n\n## The placeholder files\n\n`example_pulse_*.png` and `.wav` are installed into ComfyUI's `input/` folder the\nfirst time this pack loads. They are flat colour and a sine tone — enough to make\nthe graph open with everything resolved. **Replace them with your own** before\nrendering anything you care about; the bin rows are where you do it.\n\n## Per-shot audio, and the two modes\n\n`ref_audio` on a `PulseShot` is scene-local: visible to that shot and no other.\nThe mode beside it is what the socket is *for*, and the two are not\ninterchangeable:\n\n- **lip_sync** — the mouth matches that recording, and the clip is trimmed to the\n  window's exact span so the two describe the same seconds. Shot 2 uses this.\n- **voice_timbre** — the model speaks the shot's own written dialogue and only\n  borrows the voice's character. Shot 3 uses this.\n\nNothing about wiring the socket tells the model which you meant; the compiler\nwrites the directive into the prose, and *that sentence is the whole mechanism*.\nSet the mode without connecting audio and the pre-flight report says so.\n\n## PulseStill, feeding a shot\n\n`PulseStill` generates a single frame from the **same subject reference** the film\nuses, and hands it to Shot 1's `refs.ref_image_1`. That is the point of the node:\na shot can open on a frame generated elsewhere in this graph, and because it came\nfrom the same reference its identity already matches.\n\nNote its prompt is plain prose with no `@`. `PulseStill` has no Asset Bin — it\ntakes references on a socket, so there is no bin for a name to resolve against and\nan `@Name` there would reach the model as literal text.\n\nInside Shot 1, that arriving frame *is* addressable: scene-local references get\nshort fixed handles, `@Ref1` here, scoped to that shot alone — so every shot can\nuse the same handle without colliding.\n"
      ],
      "title": "READ ME"
    }
